<Layout title="Aftercare Guide — HUE Co." description="How to care for your permanent jewelry weld. First 24 hours, first week, ongoing care, what to avoid, and what to do if your weld breaks.">

<!-- ── Hero ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="section--dark article-hero">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <span class="eyebrow eyebrow--light">Post-Weld Care</span>
    <h1 class="section-title section-title--xl article-hero__title">Aftercare Guide</h1>
    <div class="article-hero__meta">
      <span class="article-meta__cat">Care</span>
      <span class="article-meta__sep">&middot;</span>
      <span class="article-meta__read">5 min read</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── Article Body ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="section section--cream">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <article class="article-body">

      <p class="article-lead">Permanent jewelry is designed to be worn through everything. That said, the first few days after your weld are when a little care makes the biggest difference. Here is everything you need to know.</p>

      <h2 class="article-h2">First 24 Hours</h2>
      <p>The weld is fully set as soon as you leave the studio — there's no curing time. However, the first 24 hours are when the weld is most vulnerable to disruption from friction or force.</p>
      <ul class="article-list">
        <li>Keep the area dry if possible — avoid submerging for the first 24 hours</li>
        <li>Avoid friction at the weld point: don't rub the chain against fabric repeatedly</li>
        <li>Don't tug on the chain to check if it's secure — it is</li>
        <li>Sleep on the opposite side if your chain is on a wrist or ankle</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="article-h2">First Week</h2>
      <p>Normal activity is absolutely fine after the first day. You can go back to your routine immediately after leaving the studio. A few gentle guidelines for the first week:</p>
      <ul class="article-list">
        <li>Showering is fine after 24 hours — rinse and pat dry</li>
        <li>Avoid fully submerging the weld point for 48 hours if possible (swimming, baths)</li>
        <li>Skip heavy gym work that puts repeated stress on the joint where the chain is welded — a wrist curl for a wrist chain, for example</li>
        <li>If you notice any irritation, it's usually from the chain sitting in an unfamiliar position — this passes within a few days as you adjust to the piece</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="article-h2">Ongoing Care</h2>
      <p>After the first week, your piece is ready for everything. This is what "permanent" means in practice:</p>
      <ul class="article-list">
        <li>Swim in it — ocean, pool, lake. All fine.</li>
        <li>Shower in it daily. Soap and shampoo are harmless to all three metal tiers.</li>
        <li>Sleep in it. Most clients forget they're wearing it within a week.</li>
        <li>Exercise in it. Sweat does not affect any of our metals.</li>
        <li>After ocean or pool water, a quick rinse with fresh water keeps the chain looking its best. Pat dry rather than rubbing.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="aftercare-callout">
        <p class="aftercare-callout__text">The entire point of permanent jewelry is that you don't have to think about it. Wear it everywhere.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="article-h2">What to Avoid</h2>
      <p>There are a small number of things that can degrade any fine jewelry — permanent or not:</p>
      <ul class="article-list">
        <li><strong>Harsh chemicals:</strong> Bleach, industrial cleaners, solvents. If you're working with these, wear gloves.</li>
        <li><strong>Acetone:</strong> Found in nail polish remover. Brief contact is fine; extended soaking is not. Remove nail polish away from your chain.</li>
        <li><strong>Professional steam cleaning:</strong> Do not have your piece steam-cleaned. The pressure can stress the weld. (This applies to at-home jewelry steamers as well.)</li>
        <li><strong>Ultrasonic cleaners:</strong> The vibration frequency used in these machines is not suitable for micro-welded pieces.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="article-h2">If Your Weld Breaks</h2>
      <p>It happens, occasionally. Chains can come undone if caught on something with force. Here is what to do:</p>

      <h3 class="article-h3">Within 30 Days</h3>
      <p>Your weld is under warranty. Book a re-weld appointment — it's free. Bring both pieces of the chain (the chain itself, plus the small link that came off). We'll have you back together in minutes.</p>

      <h3 class="article-h3">After 30 Days</h3>
      <p>Re-welds are available any time. The cost is $20 for Tier 1 and Tier 2, and $35 for Tier 3. Bring both pieces. The appointment takes about 15 minutes.</p>

      <h3 class="article-h3">Lost a Piece?</h3>
      <p>If you've lost the weld link or a portion of the chain, contact us before booking. In some cases we can cut and re-weld a shorter chain. In others, you may need a new chain — which comes with a full consultation and all the fun of starting fresh.</p>

      <h2 class="article-h2">Removal</h2>
      <p>Removal is always free. No questions asked. Book a removal appointment online — it takes under 60 seconds in the chair. We use a small pair of flush cutters to snip the weld link, and the chain comes off cleanly. There is no damage to your skin.</p>
      <p>If you want to keep the chain, bring a small zip bag. The chain itself is intact and can be re-welded at a future appointment ($20–$35 depending on tier).</p>
      <p>Some clients remove pieces for MRI appointments, surgeries, or when they simply want a change. Some clients have never removed a piece in three years. Both are completely fine.</p>

      <div class="article-cta">
        <h3 class="article-cta__title">Need a Re-Weld?</h3>
        <p class="article-cta__desc">Book online. We'll have you back together quickly, and the 30-day warranty resets on the new weld.</p>
        <a href="/book" class="btn btn--gold">Book Appointment</a>
      </div>

    </article>
  </div>
</section>

